WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:03.510
- [Narrator] A series of
powerful successive earthquakes

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.050
struck the Turkish-Syrian
border on February 6th.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:11.790
The first and most powerful
was a 7.8 magnitude quake.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:12.623
- This region certainly

00:00:12.623 --> 00:00:15.570
hasn't seen an earthquake
this size in centuries.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.086
- [Narrator] Destruction followed

00:00:17.086 --> 00:00:17.970
(buildings shaking)

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.610
as aftershocks hit the region.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:25.320
Then a second 7.5 magnitude
quake hit in the afternoon,

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:29.160
followed by its own series of aftershocks.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.755
In their wake, a humanitarian
and economic crisis.

00:00:32.755 --> 00:00:34.080
(building collapsing)

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.090
Thousands of people are reported dead

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.433
and even more are still missing.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.030
It's not the first time this region

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:45.450
has suffered a violent quake
and it won't be the last.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.370
To understand why, you have to look

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.720
under the surface where three
tectonic plates converge.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.850
Together, these plates cause a unique

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.420
and potentially devastating
type of earthquake.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.610
The first quake was located here,

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:02.880
11 miles under the town
of Gazientep, Turkey.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.500
11 miles may sound deep

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.500
but that's actually
shallow for a major quake.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.510
The town is especially vulnerable

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.230
because it sits near the
meeting of two tectonic plates.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.200
Tectonic plates are massive slabs of rock

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.660
on the Earth's crust from 10 miles

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.660
to 160 miles thick that
are always slowly moving.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.880
- You can take the Earth and divide it up

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.970
into several large plates
that are all moving

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.670
around each other, and
most earthquakes occur

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.310
at the boundaries between those plates.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.220
- [Narrator] This one is called
the Anatolia tectonic plate.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.080
It's actually a microplate due

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.040
to its tiny size and it's
constantly under pressure.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.110
That's because it's being pressed upward

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:49.320
against the Eurasia plate
by the Arabia plate.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.740
That pressure is squeezing
the Anatolia plate westward

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.250
where it faces even more
friction from the Africa plate,

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.200
which is also moving upward.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.600
- The Anatolian microplate is caught

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.300
in a vise between the Arabian plate moving

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.940
to the north against the Eurasian plate,

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.730
and this small plate is being pushed aside

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:10.230
as a result of that motion.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.520
- [Narrator] That means the boundaries

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.980
between the Anatolia plate and the Africa

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.160
and Arabia plates are trying
to slide against each other

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.140
as the Anatolia moves to the west

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.020
and the Africa and
Arabia move to the east.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:25.230
These borders are called
strike-slip faults

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.390
and when the friction
builds up between the plates

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:29.160
as they're pushed in different directions

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.590
until it's too much, they slip.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.960
That's a strike-slip earthquake.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.020
- The strain will build up
and finally, it will rupture

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.630
and release all that accumulated strain

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:40.890
in a large earthquake.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.480
That's what happened in Gazientep,

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.050
resulting in that 7.8 magnitude quake.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.930
USGS estimates that the
strike-slip occurred

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.210
along about 100 miles of the fault

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:55.800
about 20 miles shorter than
most strike-slip earthquakes.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.430
- These are significant
earthquakes for this fault system

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.340
and so that is probably an accumulation

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.500
of 3 to 500 years' worth

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.400
of strain that's built up
since the last earthquake.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.020
- [Narrator] Experts
say the energy released

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.820
in that slip is comparable

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.220
to that released during the explosive

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.700
1980 Mount St. Helen's volcanic eruption.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.150
The same thing happened
nine hours later in Ekinozu,

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.600
resulting in a 7.5 magnitude quake.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.510
This one was just six miles down.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:31.530
More than 285 aftershocks
have followed both quakes.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:36.530
USGS has called the area
tectonically active and unstable.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:37.620
- This region is certainly one

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.270
of the more complicated parts of the world

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:42.300
because there are four
different plates interacting,

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.440
so it becomes a region with
very complex tectonics.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.260
- [Narrator] Those complex
tectonics are responsible

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.680
for a long history of devastating quakes,

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:57.510
like this quake from 1966
that killed over 2,000 people.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:58.590
- [Reporter] Towns and villages

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.500
in 19 provinces are
almost totally destroyed.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.990
- [Narrator] The 600-mile
North Anatolian fault

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:06.870
produced seven large
strike-slip earthquakes

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.560
from 1939 to 1999,

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.100
and three earthquakes of
magnitude six or larger

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.820
have occurred within 155
miles of Monday's earthquakes

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.580
since 1970 but there hasn't been

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:23.460
a large earthquake since 1999.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.030
- Because this region hasn't
seen a large earthquake

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:29.820
for a while, it was was overdue.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:33.270
- [Narrator] That 1999
quake was a 7.4 magnitude

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:37.320
and caused an estimated 17,000 deaths.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.830
For now, USGS estimates
there could be billions

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:42.150
in economic losses.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:45.330
- The biggest thing is the
destruction and loss of life

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:49.470
and it's gonna take
them years for recovery.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.570
- [Narrator] But will
there be more disruption

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.030
if the two quakes spark a third?

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.010
Experts aren't sure.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:59.160
- It may put extra strain
on some of the nearby faults

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:02.340
or it may relieve some of the
strain on the nearby faults,

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:05.100
so it'll take some time to figure out

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.160
whether further
earthquakes are more likely

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.680
or less likely in this region.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.840
- [Narrator] But due to the
three-plate convergence,

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.500
it's not a question of if there
will be another earthquake,

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.670
it's a question of when.

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:21.337
(pensive music)

